    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full test suite is green \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented idempotent owner creation in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (the handler for `POST /api/owners`).\n\n**How it works:**\n1. **Read the header** \u2014 A new `currentIdempotencyKey()` helper reads the `Idempotency-Key` request header via `RequestContextHolder` (the generated `OwnersApi` interface signature can't be changed without regenerating from `openapi.yml`, so reading from the current request context is the clean, non-invasive route). A blank/absent header yields `null`.\n2. **Replay on repeat** \u2014 At the top of `addOwner`, if a key is present and already recorded, the originally created owner is fetched and returned with **200 OK** \u2014 skipping all the create-time rules (duplicate/identity checks that would otherwise produce a **409**).\n3. **Record on first create** \u2014 After a successful save, the `key \u2192 owner id` mapping is stored in an in-memory `ConcurrentHashMap` (`idempotentCreates`), so a subsequent request with the same key replays instead of creating a duplicate.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest.coreIdempotentRepeat` test \u2014 which posts the same structured owner twice under one key and asserts the second returns 200 with the same id \u2014 passes, and so does the rest of the suite.\n\nOne note on scope: the key store is a simple in-process map (no eviction/TTL and no request-body binding to the key), which fully satisfies the spec and tests. If you later want production-grade semantics (persistence across restarts, expiry, or rejecting a reused key with a *different* payload), that would be a follow-up.",
